  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically favoring the one with more probability mass on unfavorable outcomes (more right-skewed). The relevant information — the precise probabilities assigned to favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes — is available in the problem and could be used to assess skewness. However, the individual does not mention or appear to consider the relative distribution of probabilities beyond noting that both are 'low'. Instead, the individual collapses the probabilistic aspect by treating both as equally unlikely and then chooses based solely on payoff size. This is a different reasoning process: it is outcome-focused rather than distribution-focused. There is no indication that the individual assessed which lottery has more probability on unfavorable outcomes or used skewness as a criterion. The justification is based on maximizing payoff under perceived negligible probabilities, not on preferring more right-skewed distributions. Therefore, while both the reason and the individual might end up choosing Lottery B, the underlying logic differs fundamentally.",
