  "step_3": "The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which is A (90% chance of loss), but the individual chose B, which is less right-skewed. More importantly, the information considered by the individual (probability of avoiding loss, i.e., getting 0) is partially overlapping with the data needed for skewness (probabilities and outcomes), but their focus is on maximizing the chance of the favorable outcome (0), which is the opposite of what a right-skewness preference entails (which would favor higher probability on unfavorable outcomes). The justification is based on wanting a higher chance to avoid loss, not on preferring a skewed distribution. There is no indication that the individual evaluated the shape of the distribution or conceptualized one lottery as more skewed than the other. Therefore, the reasoning process contradicts the decision reason. The individual’s logic aligns with risk aversion or maximizing the chance of the best outcome, not with preferring right-skewness.",
